Package: com.example.multifloat
Name: MultiFloat
Version: 0.9.1
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Multi-floating window / split-view tweak for iOS 17 (roothide / arm64e). 0.9.1 fixes the single remaining defect reported on device in 0.9.0 (archive #9): switching apps no longer blacks the hosted content out, but it "blinks" for a moment before the picture comes back. The archive #9 log pinned the timing exactly (same pid, one hosting round): at t+8488 the scene's deactivationReasons goes 0 -> 33 while the picture is still there, at t+8489 contentState 2 -> 0 and layerManager.layers 2 -> 0 (that is the blink), and at t+8490 the keep-alive pulls it back so contentState returns to 2 with 2 layers. In other words the black window lasts about one keep-alive period, which makes keepAliveMs literally the length of the blink: the default is lowered from 1000 ms to 200 ms. Archive #9 also exposed a deeper trap. `fg` reads 0 in every single log line, including right after the P0 step wrote it and right after every keep-alive pull. The truth is that UIMutableApplicationSceneSettings has no setForeground: / foreground on this device at all, and because every helper is guarded by respondsToSelector the entire foreground path had been silently a no-op since 0.7.0. 0.9.0's drop detection condition (!fg && fixFg) was therefore always true, which is why the log showed "pull #10 / #20 / #30 ...": it was firing an updateSettingsWithBlock every second for nothing. 0.9.1 therefore stops treating foreground as evidence: the drop condition now uses only quantities confirmed to exist here (layerManager.layers <= 0, contentState != 2, deactivationReasons != 0, idleModeEnabled == 1), foreground is demoted to a plain log field, and the keep-alive only issues a settings update when the scene is genuinely bad. It also reports how many periods each drop lasted, converted to milliseconds, which is the only hard evidence of how long the blink is. The capability report now prints a responds-matrix for every property around this judgement (foreground/level/frame/idleModeEnabled/deactivationReasons/interfaceOrientation/displayConfiguration/updateSettingsWithBlock: on both the settings object and the FBScene) plus the full ivar list of UIApplication, because the next step towards removing the blink entirely is acquiring a UIApplicationSceneDeactivationAssertion so SpringBoard never deactivates the hosted scene in the first place. Pull-back is now per-item switchable (keepAliveDeact, keepAliveIdle, keepAliveLevel, keepAliveForeground), so one device run reveals the minimum set that has to be maintained. Still no hooks are installed anywhere: only updateSettingsWithBlock:. Config defaults (override in /var/mobile/MultiFloat.plist): hostRoute 3, hostSceneFrame true, resizesHosted true, forceForeground true, keepAlive true, keepAliveMs 200, keepAliveDeact true, keepAliveIdle true, keepAliveLevel true, keepAliveForeground false, allowRouteB false, hostStep 6. cfgVersion=10, migrated once on first launch (keepAliveMs 1000 -> 200 and keepAliveForeground true -> false, only when they still hold the old defaults). DISABLED BY DEFAULT for real hosting: needs enabled=true AND hostEnabled=true. Crash-guard marker still makes a crash loop impossible. Log: /var/mobile/MultiFloat.log
Maintainer: QingC12 <QingC12@users.noreply.github.com>
Author: QingC12
Section: Tweaks
Depends: firmware (>= 15.0)
